{
  "dom_id": "step-q115754",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/34af8d1078100aea331c300ea529d2fdefbdd31e99ee8a7946325d58a8abf680.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=7-{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{3}{x+7}$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069297/assets/d02883b776dbcd55c5c96347ed1a7bb0e51ef8c26e62786049b6585b878d04fb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-7.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=7-{x}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -7.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(1+\\frac{7}{x})}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{3}{x+7}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{x+7}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
